WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.625
Two federal documents frame
the reset and recovery path.

2
00:00:03.625 --> 00:00:09.261
OMB's zero trust memo requires
phishing-resistant MFA, and then names the hole:

3
00:00:09.261 --> 00:00:12.344
recovery is a potential
bypass of all that,

4
00:00:12.344 --> 00:00:15.162
and a serious threat if it isn't handled.

5
00:00:15.162 --> 00:00:19.228
It asks for high-friction methods
that are costly for an attacker,

6
00:00:19.228 --> 00:00:21.412
like in-person checks or live video.

7
00:00:21.412 --> 00:00:25.601
NIST's digital identity guidelines
give recovery its own section.

8
00:00:25.601 --> 00:00:30.442
They allow a help desk agent only on
the basis of a documented risk analysis,

9
00:00:30.442 --> 00:00:33.332
repeated proofing has
to match the original,

10
00:00:33.332 --> 00:00:36.612
and every recovery must
notify the account holder.

11
00:00:36.612 --> 00:00:41.336
Put together, the help desk reset should
be the exception, not the main path,

12
00:00:41.336 --> 00:00:46.137
at least as strong as the original
proofing, and always followed by a notice.

13
00:00:46.137 --> 00:00:49.975
Email, caller ID and knowledge
questions verify no one.

14
00:00:49.975 --> 00:00:52.700
Now look at the same problem
from the compliance side.
